  "preamble": "Devoicing of the voiced alveolar/dental stop [d] to [t] is one of the most widespread sound changes. It occurs as an unconditioned context-free shift (Grimm's law in Germanic, the Armenian consonant shift, Yeniseian), as the loss of the Middle Chinese voiced series in New Xiang and Cantonese and of Proto-Hmong-Mien voiced initials in Hmong, where the lost voicing survives as a low tone register, and as positional word-final devoicing (German Auslautverhärtung). Bru (Katuic) likewise devoiced *d with a breathy register on the vowel (Proto-Katuic *ʔadaa 'duck' > ti̤a).",
  "phoneticEffects": "Devoicing, Register Split, Tonogenesis, Final Devoicing",
  "languageExamples": [
    {
      "language": "Proto-Germanic",
      "languageFamily": "Germanic",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "PIE *déḱm̥ 'ten'",
          "to": "*tehun (Gothic taihun, Old English tīen)",
          "note": "Grimm's law: PIE voiced stops become voiceless; cf. Latin decem."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "language": "German",
      "languageFamily": "West Germanic",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "Rad- [ʁaːd-] (gen. Rades, pl. Räder) 'wheel'",
          "to": "Rad [ʁaːt]",
          "note": "Word-final devoicing (Auslautverhärtung); /d/ surfaces before a vowel-initial suffix."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "language": "Classical Armenian",
      "languageFamily": "Armenian",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "PIE *déḱm̥ 'ten'",
          "to": "տասն tasn",
          "note": "Armenian consonant shift: PIE voiced stops devoice; cf. Latin decem."
        },
        {
          "from": "PIE *dṓm 'house'",
          "to": "տուն tun 'house'"
        },
        {
          "from": "PIE *deh₃- 'to give'",
          "to": "տամ tam 'I give'"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "language": "Xiang Chinese (Changsha)",
      "languageFamily": "Sinitic",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "桃 Middle Chinese daw 'peach'",
          "to": "Changsha taɤ (tone 2)",
          "note": "New Xiang devoices Middle Chinese voiced initials to plain voiceless stops; Old Xiang Shuangfeng də2 and Shaoyang daɤ2 keep the voiced initial (Norman 1988 table)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "language": "Cantonese",
      "languageFamily": "Sinitic",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "大 Middle Chinese dajH 'big'",
          "to": "daai6 /taːi˨˨/",
          "note": "Middle Chinese voiced initials devoice (unaspirated in non-level tones); the former voicing survives as the low (yang) tone register."
        }
      ]
    },
